# The leagues' dashboard

`leagues/dashboard.py` is a read-only web page over the new league system's files, as `FORMATS.md` defines them.
It uses only the standard library, reads files and `/proc`, and writes nothing, except on the Play page (below,
since 2026-11-12): that page runs one engine process and writes under `leagues/play-games/` only. Pages refresh in
place every 30 s (the Play page doesn't).

## Running it

```
cd /home/iv/dev/chessai/leagues
setsid nohup nice -n 19 python3 dashboard.py >> dashboard.log 2>&1 < /dev/null &
```

- `--root DIR`: the root that holds `index.json`, `global/` and `experiments/`. The default is this file's directory
  (the real root). For the smoke data, use `--root /home/iv/dev/chessai/leagues-smoke`.
- `--port N`: the default is 8766. It listens on 127.0.0.1 only.
- `--old-dashboard URL`: the champion run's dashboard, linked for the champion run and the first run. The default
  is `http://127.0.0.1:8765/`; `""` drops the links.
- `--once PATH`: renders one page (`/`, `/x/`, `/m/`, `/play/`, `/global/`, `/x/NAME/`, `/a/`, `/a/PATH/`,
  `/a/PATH/h2h`) to stdout and exits. Use it for checks.

The server lowers itself to nice 19. Stop it by its pid; the start line in `dashboard.log` prints it.

## Pages

The sticky bar at the top holds the navigation and the filters.

- **Navigation**: the menu (since 2026-11-12), Home, Experiments, Arenas, Models and Play, each a page, then the
  earlier runs:
  - Home (`/`): the global ladder and the menu;
  - Experiments (`/x/`), then the running experiments as links (and the one the page shows), and a list of all of
    them;
  - Arenas (`/a/`), then the running arenas as links (and the one the page shows, a ladder run's too), and a list of
    all of them; the ladder's runs are left out of the list (they are on Home);
  - Models (`/m/`): the model library;
  - Play (`/play/`): a game against a library model;
  - links to the champion run and the first run on the champion run's own dashboard (port 8765).

  Each link has a status dot: green running, amber waiting or paused, blue done, red failed, grey otherwise.
- **Filters**: chips per factor, colour-by, the highlighted line, and how many lines show.
  - They drive every panel on the page: table rows, chart series, legends, the grid's cells and an arena's
    head-to-head rows and columns.
  - Factors with only one value on the page are left out.
  - Experiments add a factor per overridden flag (a sweep's `lr-rate`, say), the replicate and the base model.
    Colour-by starts on the swept flag when there is one, else on the option (several), else on the model.
  - Experiments with observers (below) add the Role factor first: participants (play), observers (train only). It
    shows or hides the observers on every panel. A flag that only an observer's recipe differs in is a factor too,
    but colour-by starts on a flag only when the participants sweep it.
  - Each kind of page (experiments, arenas, models, global, experiment, arena) keeps its own filter state in the
    browser. The Experiments page keeps the state saved while its tables were Home's.
  - Click a row, a cell, a legend entry or a chart line to highlight that line everywhere on the page. Click it
    again to clear it.
- **Tables** sort when you click a heading, which cycles through descending, ascending and off. Sort order survives
  the refresh.
- **Arrows**: an Elo change (since the previous cycle, since an anchor locked) or a gap to the champion has a green
  ▲ when up and a red ▼ when down, none when it rounds to 0. The numbers are unchanged. Gaps between options get
  none, since neither direction is better.

### Home `/`

Since 2026-11-12 (the user: "I feel like the dashboard is getting cluttered. I wish we could split it into a home
page (Global Ladder, and menu) In the menu: experiments, arenas, models"), Home holds:

- the menu: a tile per page with its counts: Experiments (by status), Arenas (how many run), Models (the models the
  Models page shows by default, of the library's), and Play;
- **Global ladder**: the global ladder (below), or "No global ladder yet" while `arenas/ladder/` holds no arena.

It has no filters. Links to Home's old sections (`/#experiments`, `/#recent`, `/#archives`, `/#arenas`) lead to
their new pages. Before 2026-11-12 Home also held the Experiments, Arenas, Recent events and Earlier runs sections
(`dashboard.py.before-pages`).

### Experiments `/x/`

Home's sections until 2026-11-12, Experiments, then Recent events and Earlier runs:

- **Experiments**: one row per experiment, each name a link. Columns:
  - status: checked against `/proc`, so a `running` or `waiting` league whose driver is gone shows as `stopped`,
    with the reason in the tooltip; a STOP file is noted there too;
  - now: the running cycle's phase, and during the tournament its rounds played of those planned;
  - cycle n of N, with a meter;
  - lines, as models × options, then the observers ("4 × 1: 4 lines, 2 observers"; their models in the tooltip),
    then "rated cold" for an experiment with cold_games (its temperature, share and train in the tooltip), then
    "filtered" for one with train_filter (its rule in the tooltip), then "openings" for one with openings (how its
    games open in the tooltip), then "rated every N" for one with rate_every (its schedule in the tooltip);
  - started: cycle 1's start;
  - finished: done experiments' finish time; for running ones, a projected time;
  - prices: the prices version the league runs on (a self-play-only experiment's budget in the tooltip);
  - CPU spent, measured, broken down in the tooltip (with the tournament games' price beside it);
  - the leading line: the line whose current version rates highest at ply 2.
- **Recent events**: the latest 40 events across every league.
- **Earlier runs**: the global league's row (the Experiments columns; its page stays at `/global/`), then the
  links below.
- Filters: kind (shown once the global league exists), status, model, option. They apply to the Experiments rows,
  the recent events and the global league's row.

Leagues on disk that `index.json` doesn't list are shown too, with "not in index.json" in the status tooltip.

### The global ladder (Home, since 2026-11-12)

The user, 2026-11-12 ~13:20: "Can we make the global ladder separate from the arena (replace the original global
ladder that was stopped with 2 final competitors". The ladder's runs are the arenas under `arenas/ladder/`
(`leagues/specs/arenas/make_ladder.py` writes their specs, `arena.py` runs them; no model trains). Home's Global
ladder section shows:
- the latest run (by `created`): a line with its status, players (models, each at its own specified ply; depth-anchor
  players; random references), round-robins and creation, and how its Elo is anchored (random = 0, or the pinned or
  refitted scale the arena page would show);
- its standings, highest Elo first, one row per player: the model as experiment, base model and replicate, version
  (the arena entry and its label in the tooltip); a "depth anchor" badge when the player plays at a ply other than
  the one its model was trained at (the label's "train ply N"); the random references muted; ply, Elo, ± (95%),
  games;
- every ladder run, as the Arenas list shows arenas.

It ranks players (a model with its search depth), so the plies mix by design; single-ply panel reads judge
evaluators. A run's own page is its arena page (`/a/ladder/<name>/`), titled "Global ladder <name>". The ladder's
runs are not in the Arenas lists or counts. The global league it replaces (`global/`, stopped 2026-11-12 09:36) is
under Earlier runs.

### A league: `/global/` and `/x/<name>/`

The heading names the models (by label), the options, the cycle, the prices version and, for a self-play-only
experiment, its fixed self-play budget.

**Priced and measured CPU.** Since FORMATS.md's 16:40 entry every budget is priced CPU: a game's price comes from
its plies and the constants of `leagues/prices.csv`, frozen in the league's config, so a league replays exactly.
Wherever a budget or a data amount is shown (training data, data targets and budgets, copied global rounds, the
batch, rounds), the priced figure leads and says "priced"; the measured CPU stands beside it or in the tooltip, for
information. What a league spent (the CPU tile, the Cycles chart and its CPU columns) is measured CPU. A league made
before prices shows measured CPU throughout.

Panels, top to bottom. The global league has no "By model and option", "Training data" or "Global rounds
copied" panels; experiments have no "Anchors" or "Latest batch" panels.

**Observers** (added 2026-11-12; the user: "let's add observers to the dashboard also"). An experiment's observers
(FORMATS.md, config.json: lines with `"observer": true`, status `observer`) train each cycle on the participants'
local set and never play. They have no in-run rating, so the pages show their versions and training, never an Elo:

- the heading names them after the cycle ("· observers: F-P ×2", labels without the replicate);
- Now: the Lines tile counts the participants; its subtitle adds the observers;
- By model and option, By setting and Gaps between options: participants only;
- Elo by cycle: participants only (the legend too); the note says why;
- Standings stays one flat table: after the field's entries, each observer at its latest version with an
  `observer` badge in the Role column, "—" for Elo, ±, change and games, and its trained games and positions
  (`observers.csv`, summed from version 0);
- Lines: status `observer`, its latest version marked "latest; never plays", no champion, gap, z, decision or
  crownings, its last training's data from `observers.csv`;
- **Observers** (a panel after Lines, only with observers): one row per observer (latest version, trainings, last
  trained cycle, trained games and positions, "As the participants": the cycles in which it trained on exactly the
  participants' set, training CPU, data, recipe), then `observers.csv` per cycle, newest first (the latest 120
  cycles): version, new version, train games and positions, same as the participants or not, training CPU, data;
- Training data: a column and a chart line per observer, from `observers.csv`; not in the per-option average;
- Cycles: an Observers column and a stack in the chart (`totals.csv` `observer_train_cpu_s`), counted in CPU;
- the CPU tile and the Experiments page's CPU include the observers' training;
- a tree or DAG observer has its nodes link (Lines, Observers); its node report says it is an observer, and every
  report of the experiment lists the observers apart from the field.

An arena entry that is an observer's version (`<experiment>:<line>.obs@<v>`) gets the badge in the arena's
standings, and its trained games and positions come from that experiment's `observers.csv`.

**Cold games** (added 2026-11-12). An experiment with cold_games (FORMATS.md, config.json) is rated on its cold
round-robins alone, so its pages say which games rate it:

- the heading after the cycle: "· rated on cold games, temperature 0.02, 25% of the cycle's games; they train too"
  (or "; rating only");
- Elo by cycle and Standings: their notes say the Elo is the cold games' fit and that the round-robins' own fit
  (tour/) decides nothing;
- Cycles: a Cold games column (the cycle's cold games; rounds, CPU, process CPU, price and the games that joined the
  local set in the tooltip), a Cold CPU column and a stack in the chart (`totals.csv` `cold_proc_cpu_s`: their
  games, fit and tables), counted in CPU;
- the CPU tile and the Experiments page's CPU include the cold games;
- the cycle reports have a Cold games line.

**Train filter** (added 2026-11-12 12:42; the rule's text revised for the user's rule of 13:22-13:24). An
experiment with train_filter, usually with self_games_topup (FORMATS.md, config.json), trains each line on its own
set: the games whose players are all rated at or above its floor (its current cold Elo minus its standard error, or
minus a fixed margin), its own games too, topped up with games against its champion or itself:

- the heading after the cycle: "· each line trains on the games whose players are all rated at or above its current
  cold Elo minus its standard error, its own games too; topped up with games against its champion or itself"; the
  Experiments page's lines cell adds "filtered", with the same text in its tooltip;
- Training data: each line's cell tooltip adds its filter (cycles.csv's `filter_*` and `topup_*` columns): its
  rating, SE and floor, the games it kept (and how many of its own games) and their shares of the local set's games
  and positions, and its top-up games and their opponent (its champion, or itself);
- Cycles: a Top-up games column (the games the filter cut and from how many lines' sets, the top-up games'
  positions and price in the tooltip) and a Top-up CPU column with a stack in the chart (`totals.csv`
  `topup_proc_cpu_s`), counted in CPU;
- the CPU tile and the Experiments page's CPU include the top-up games;
- the cycle reports have a Train filter line.

**Openings** (added 2026-11-12). An experiment with openings (FORMATS.md, config.json) opens its round-robins,
self-games and top-up games with K random plies (the cold games don't): the heading after the cycle says "· games
open with 6 random plies (not the cold games)"; the Experiments page's lines cell adds "openings", with the same
text in its tooltip.
Nothing else changes: its games, ratings and records read as any experiment's (the opening plies are in the games'
plies).

**Rate every N** (added 2026-11-12). An experiment with rate_every (FORMATS.md, config.json) plays its cold games and
decides on cycles 1, N + 1, 2N + 1, ... only; the cycles between play and train, and their records have no Elo. Its
pages take every rating and decision from the rated cycles, never from an unrated one:

- the heading after the cycle: "· rated every 4 cycles (1, 5, 9, ...; the others play and train only)"; the
  Experiments page's lines cell adds "rated every 4", with the same text in its tooltip, and its leader is the last
  rated cycle's;
- Now: a Rated cycles tile (every N; the last rated cycle and the next one), and while an unrated cycle runs a note
  that it doesn't rate (no cold games, no champion test, no crowning, no plateau check); the Field tile is the last
  rated cycle's;
- By model and option: the last rated cycle's ratings and the change since the rated cycle before;
- Elo by cycle: one point per rated cycle, consecutive rated cycles joined (an unrated cycle has no point and makes
  no gap), with a note saying so;
- Standings: the last rated cycle's field (its note says so); Change against the previous rated cycle;
- Lines: "Last rated" (the version that played the last rated cycle, with its Elo) in place of "Last played", Gap,
  z and Decision from that cycle (the decision with its cycle: "champion kept · cycle 9"), and a Champion age column
  after Decision: the cycles since the line's champion was crowned (state.json `champion_cycle`; crownings happen on
  rated cycles only);
- Training data: in an unrated cycle a filtered line's tooltip says its rating, SE and floor are the last rated
  cycle's;
- Cycles: a Rated column ("rated" or a muted "unrated"; totals.csv `rated`), and "(unrated)" in the chart's tooltips;
- the cycle reports have a Rated cycle or Unrated cycle line.

- **Now**: tiles for:
  - status and phase, with the driver's pid and uptime;
  - cycles done (n of N, with a meter), and the running cycle's start and round;
  - lines;
  - the field: entries, anchors, references;
  - CPU spent (measured);
  - experiments: the data budget, priced: a self-play-only experiment's fixed budget per line per cycle, else
    the last training cycle's target (the price of its qualifying games);
  - the last cycle's wall time;
  - experiments: time left;
  - experiments with global data: global rounds copied (priced), time waited, and the global carry: how far the
    global lines' data leads or trails the local lines' (`state.json` `global_carry_s`).

  While a cycle runs, a phase strip shows the cycle's phases in the order the driver runs them:
  - global: calibration, tournament, batch, decide, train, records;
  - experiments: copy (only with the global option; it reads "copy: waiting for global rounds" while the
    experiment waits), calibration, tournament, decide, train, self-play (only with a self-play line), records.

  Earlier phases have a tick and the current one is highlighted; the tournament shows its rounds played of those
  planned. In an experiment's last planned cycle, copy, train and self-play are struck through: that cycle copies
  and trains nothing. During the tournament, a note below the strip gives the cycle's rounds, games and CPU so far
  (`rounds.csv`). The last 14 lines of `driver.log` sit in a fold.
- **Entry ply** (experiments with `entry_ply` other than 2 in config.json: 1, 3 or 4 so far): the panels below say
  "ply 2" for the main entries; on such a page they say ply P instead (the grid, the gaps, the Elo chart's single
  ply, the Lines table's and the standings' tooltips, the rounds' qualifying games). The champion-gap tooltips name
  the entry ply too: current@P minus champion@P (until 2026-11-11 01:38 a ply-1 page said current@2 there). A
  warm-started line (config.json `lines[].start`, from 2026-11-11 03:27) shows like any other; its "entered" event
  names the start model and its md5, but the trained-games tooltips still call its version 0 "the untrained version
  0".
- **By model and option** (experiments): a grid of models (rows) by options (columns). Each cell shows:
  - the line's current version at ply 2, with its ±;
  - the change since the previous cycle;
  - how many new champions it has had;
  - a "best" mark on the model's best option.

  Cells the filters exclude are dimmed rather than hidden, so the columns stay aligned.

  Models show by label (`F-L lr-rate=0.025 #2` for the id `F-L+lr-rate=0.025#2`), and lines as the label plus
  the option suffix.

  With flag overrides or replicates, **By setting** groups the lines by setting (the base model and its overrides)
  and option: the replicates' mean ply-2 rating in the last cycle, their spread, the mean over the last 5 cycles,
  and each replicate's rating.

  Below the grid, **Gaps between options**: for each model and each pair of options:
  - the last cycle's ply-2 gap in words, with the two ±s combined as if independent;
  - the mean gap over the last 5 cycles;
  - how many cycles each option was ahead.
- **Elo by cycle**: each line's current version per cycle, from `cycles.csv`.
  - Toggles: ply 2 or ply 1; the champions (dotted, the champion before each decision); the references; the rings
    where a version was crowned. The rings are on by default up to 12 lines; the browser remembers your choices.
  - In experiments, lines that share a colour get dashes from the first of replicate, model and option that isn't
    the colour: a sweep coloured by its flag dashes its replicates. The legend shows each line's colour and dash.
- **Standings**: every entry in the last completed cycle's field (`history.csv`), by Elo. Columns:
  - line;
  - version name (`G.x.y`, `G.x.yF`; experiments `E.S.x.y`, `E.L.x.y`, `E.G.x.y` by option), with the entry id
    in the tooltip;
  - ply and role;
  - Elo and ±;
  - change since the previous cycle. For a current entry, it is measured against the line's previous version at
    the same ply. For any other entry, it is the entry's own move;
  - games;
  - trained games and trained positions (added 2026-11-11; the user asked for them): what the entry's version was
    trained on, summed from the line's untrained version 0 (0, 0) along its lineage. Every version trains on from
    the line's previous one (leagues.py train_line), and the cycles.csv row whose next_version is the version + 1
    made it; a failed or skipped training isn't counted. Local and global lines count their training set
    (train_games, train_positions: the round robins' games, plus the self-games where the experiment plays them);
    self-play lines count their self-play games and plies (selfplay_games, selfplay_plies). Shown as 45.2k / 12.3M /
    1.7B, sorted by the exact number, which the cell's tooltip gives with the number of trainings. "—" where
    cycles.csv lacks a count (no experiment does at present); blank for references. In a local experiment every
    local line trains on the same set each cycle, so the counts differ mainly between champions;
  - for current ply-2 entries, the gap to the champion in words, with z.
- **Lines**: one row per line; a tree or DAG line (config kind `bdt` or `bdd`) has a **nodes** link after its
  name, to its node report (below). Columns:
  - status (retired, with its cycle);
  - the version that plays next (— once retired);
  - the last played version with its ply-2 Elo;
  - the champion with its Elo;
  - the gap and z;
  - the last decision;
  - global: patience n of 20;
  - new champions crowned;
  - the cycle it entered;
  - experiments: the priced data of its last training cycle (measured in the tooltip);
  - the recipe: training arguments. The tooltip has the recipe, the binaries, the held-out set, a self-play line's
    carry and mean round price (priced) and games per round, and a variant's model id and replicate.
- **Anchors** (global): one row per 100-Elo interval, highest first, including empty intervals up to the best
  current ply-1 entry. Columns:
  - the anchor's name and line;
  - when it locked and at what Elo;
  - its Elo now and how far it has moved;
  - a mini chart of its rating against its interval and margins;
  - its streak outside the interval;
  - locked or available.
- **Events**: `events.csv`, newest first (the latest 250), with counts per event type. Covers champions, retirements,
  anchors, copies, waits and done; new champions are in green.
- **Training data, priced CPU** (experiments):
  - a chart of each line's training data per cycle (`cycles.csv` `data_price_s`) against the cycle's target
    (dashed, `totals.csv` `data_price_target_s`), or against the fixed budget in a self-play-only experiment
    (`config.json` `selfplay_budget_s`);
  - the average per option, relative to the budget;
  - a table of cycles × lines, each cell a meter with its % of the target. A self-play line is measured against its
    own budget (`selfplay_budget_s`: the target or fixed budget plus its carry). Its tooltip has the priced
    generation, the rounds, games and plies, the carry after the cycle, and the measured generation and job CPU.
    The last cycle of an experiment trains nothing and shows "—".
- **Global rounds copied** (experiments with the global option):
  - where the rounds come from (linked to the global league when it is this root's);
  - rounds used, from `state.json`;
  - the global carry (priced), and per cycle the copied qualifying data against the local data, both priced;
  - waits, from the wait-start and wait-end events, including an open wait;
  - `copied.csv`, newest first: priced (the experiment's prices) and measured CPU of each round.
- **Cycles**: a stacked bar chart of measured CPU per cycle (tournament games, rating fit, training, self-play,
  calibration), and `totals.csv` newest first (the latest 120 cycles). Columns:
  - wall time, field, rounds, games;
  - tournament CPU (the games' own, measured), the games' price, and measured CPU per round;
  - rating fit: the tournament process's own CPU beyond its games (`tournament_proc_cpu_s` minus
    `tournament_cpu_s`), the column shown once a cycle has recorded it;
  - training, self-play and calibration CPU, and the total (the rating fit included);
  - new champions;
  - global: retired lines and anchors;
  - experiments: the data target (priced; not in self-play-only experiments, whose budget is fixed), global
    rounds (priced and measured in the tooltip) and time waited.
- **Rounds**: a bar chart of priced CPU per round over the latest 250 rounds, split into qualifying games and the
  rest; the measured CPU is in the tooltips. Below it, the last three cycles' rounds from `rounds.csv`: priced and
  qualifying priced, measured and qualifying measured, and measured CPU per game.
- **Latest batch** (global): `batch/batch.json`'s cycle, when it was made, its prices version and its rounds,
  priced (the global league's prices) and measured. Each round lists the experiments whose `state.json` used it.
- **Reports and log**: the latest `cycle-NNN.md` in a fold, every report as text (`report/NNN`), the last 256 KB of
  `driver.log` (`log`), and `config.json` and `state.json` (`file/...`).

### Arenas `/a/`

- **Batch 4: rerun minus original**: one row per finished pair, from `arenas/pairs/summary.csv`. Columns:
  - the pair arena (`arenas/pairs/<original>`), the original and the rerun (links to their experiments);
  - lines: the lines both runs have, both champions rated;
  - rerun − original at ply 2: the mean over those lines with its 95% interval, as `summary.csv` has it;
  - ply 1: the same mean from the pair's ply-1 replay (the arena named `ply1-<original>`) where one exists, computed
    the way pairs.py's report computes ply 2;
  - a forest plot of both (● ply 2, ○ ply 1, each with its interval; one scale for every row, the line at 0);
  - cycles and CPU hours, the original's / the rerun's; the arena's round-robins × games; when it finished.

  Below the table, the pairs not run yet, in pairs.py's `PAIRS` order, each with its state: its rerun not created
  yet, running at cycle c, held (`OPTIONAL`, e.g. `selfplay-depth`), its arena running, or failed.
- **Every arena**: one row per arena but the global ladder's (`arenas/ladder/`, under Global ladder on Home),
  running ones first, then the newest: its directory (a link), status, players (how many are references),
  round-robins of the stop rule's most, games, CPU, created, description.
- Filters (since 2026-11-12): status, ply (its entries' plies) and folder (its directory under `arenas/`: diag,
  pairs, ply1, top level). They apply to Every arena; the pairs table has none.

### An arena `/a/<path>/`

`<path>` is the arena's directory under `arenas/`, e.g. `/a/pairs/lr-settings/` or `/a/matched-depth/`. Without
the trailing slash it redirects; a directory that only holds arenas (`/a/pairs/`) is a 404 that lists them. The
heading carries the status and `config.json`'s description, then:

- **Now**: tiles for the status (with the phase: own calibration games or round-robins), the round-robins of the
  stop rule's most, the players, the games, the CPU (the chunks' CPU and wall time in `state.json`), the widest 95%
  interval (the stop rule's measure), the calibrations identical to their experiment's, and the binary.
  `arena.log`'s last lines are in a fold.
- **Rerun minus original** (pair arenas, `pair-<exp>` and `ply1-<exp>`): one row per matched line, with both
  champions (version, when crowned) and the rerun's minus the original's at ply 2 and at ply 1, each with its 95%
  interval and a forest plot. The last row is the mean over the matched lines. Ply 2 comes from `report.csv` and
  `pairs/summary.csv` when they exist. Ply 1, and ply 2 before its report exists, are computed from the games with
  pairs.py's `contrast_ses` (the fit's covariance), as its report computes them.
- **Standings**: the players as `arena.py status` lists them (`tour/ratings.csv`, highest Elo first): rank, player
  (an entry by its name, without the ply), ply, Elo, ±, games, score, trained games, trained positions, positions
  (when entries record them), label. References are muted and marked.
  - Trained games and trained positions (added 2026-11-11; the user asked for them, for more about the models
    brought into an arena): the entry's source version, counted from its experiment's cycles.csv exactly as that
    experiment's Standings count it (above), in the same format. A version entry's source names the version
    (`<experiment>:<line>@<version>`); a file entry counts only when its model is that version's own file,
    `experiments/<experiment>/runs/<line>/<the line's kind><version>.model` (the diag arenas). The tooltip names
    the version looked up. Blank for the references; "—" for an entry that isn't an experiment version
    (matched-depth's `.round@` models in `models/from-scratch/matched-depth/`) or a version cycles.csv doesn't
    record.
- **Head to head**: every player against every other, references included, in Standings order, with each
  player's Elo beside its row.
  - A cell is the row player's total against the column player over all the round-robins (two games a pair each):
    wins–losses–draws, the score, the Elo with its 95% interval (arena.py's `score_interval`, as
    `arena.py status --vs` prints it), or the games. The buttons above the table choose which; the browser keeps
    the choice.
  - The colour is the row player's score on a nine-step scale, red below 50%, blue above, grey around 50%.
  - Hover a cell for all of it, with the expected score from the two ratings and how far over or under it the
    result is.
  - Click a row's name for that player's results against each opponent; click it again or ✕ to close.
  - The table is a separate fragment (`h2h`), fetched again only when the tournament's files change. The filters
    hide the rows and columns of the players they leave out.
- **Calibration**: `calibration.csv`, each entry's own calibration (fitted on its own games) beside its
  experiment's ("the same" when identical).
- **Report and log**: `report.md` in a fold (pairs), FAILED's first line, and the files as text.
- Filters: experiment, line, role (entry or reference), and ply when there are several. Colour by experiment.

Routes under an arena's page:

- `h2h`: the head-to-head table (an HTML fragment);
- `h2h/player?name=<player>`: one player's results against each opponent (an HTML fragment);
- `file/<name>`: the end of a file as text: `config.json`, `state.json`, `calibration.csv`, `report.md`,
  `report.csv`, `arena.log`, `run.out`, `DONE`, `FAILED`, `tour/ratings.md`, `tour/head_to_head.md`,
  `tour/tournament.log`.

### A line's node report `/x/<name>/nodes/<line>` (and `/global/nodes/<line>`)

A tree or DAG line's node report, made by `leagues/treeview.py` (its docstring says what the page shows). The page
is cached by the md5 of the line's model file and treeview.py's `TOOL_VERSION`, in
`models/from-scratch/treeview-cache/pages/<md5>-v<version>.html`: a request for the current version (or `?v=N`)
whose page exists gets it at once. Otherwise the dashboard starts treeview.py in the background (`nice -n 15`, one
report at a time, its output in `<md5>-v<version>.log`, its pid in `<md5>-v<version>.pid`) and answers
"Generating…", which reloads every 5 s for that version (the line moves on while the league plays) until the page is
there, 5-10 s for a nine-leaf model on 200k positions. A run that failed shows its log with a "try again" link
(`?v=N&retry=1`). The games are the league's latest finished rounds (see the docstring); the page is never
regenerated for the same model file and tool version. The dashboard reads `TOOL_VERSION` from treeview.py at each
request, so raising it (whenever the pages change) makes new pages without a restart; older pages stay on disk,
unused. Nothing else on any page changed: every other page was checked byte-identical to the code before
(`dashboard.py.before-treeview`) on 2026-11-11, but for the links and the home page's clock.

DAG pages with path context (version 3, 2026-11-11 15:45; `dashboard.py.before-tvversion` is the code before the
version key): a top line "path parameters N; log-loss with X, without Y; difference ± SE", the model's log-loss
with all its path cells and with all of them neutral (every router routing by its classifier alone, every leaf
predicting by its own), the per-position difference's standard error paired over positions and clustered by game.
Each node with cells gets a "Path parameters" part: one table per cell (an upstream sibling set) with a row per
arrival (through member j, or not through the set): the share of the node's positions, the cell's terms (LR leaf:
the weights per result and white minus black; probit leaf: the latent weight's mean and SD, and the mean change it
makes to the white-minus-black log-odds; router: the centred shift to each child's log routing score, and the share
of those positions it routes elsewhere without the cell); its eight commonest paths from the root with their total
offset and effect, the rest lumped; and its payoff, the model's log-loss and winner log-loss on its positions with
its cells and with them neutral (a router: the from-to matrix of its neutral choice against its choice with cells,
and the log-loss of the positions it moves). From `chessai-treeview2 path-detail`, whose predictions with cells
were checked bit for bit against `chessai-errdag4 eval-loss --dump`.

### Models `/m/`

The model library (`leagues/library`, LIBRARY.md) as a page, added 2026-11-12. The user: "I would like to be able
to sort models by model type, but also by several other criteria like: self-play trained vs homogenous class vs
diverse class vs other, ply trained on. Only models that fully plateaued should be there I think plus any holdouts
from previous experiments if we find them interesting or impressive."

- **Rows**: one per `library/index.csv` row (a line's final version; the global league's best and last champions),
  plus one per holdout of a version the index doesn't have. Such a row is made from its line's row and marked
  "holdout version"; its tooltip says whether the library has a snapshot of that version.
- **Shown by default**: the fully plateaued models (index.csv `plateaued` yes: the plateau rule ended the run, and
  the row is the line's final version) and the holdouts. The buttons "Plateaued + holdouts" and "All models" set the
  Set filter; "clear all" shows every row.
- **Holdouts**: `library/holdouts.csv`, columns `id,reason`, curated by hand (the page only reads it). An id is an
  index id (every row with it; `global_f` has two), `<id>@<version>`, or `<experiment>:<line>@<version>` (an arena
  source). A holdout has a "holdout" badge with its reason. Holdouts that match nothing are listed above the table.
- **Columns**, every one sortable:
  - model: the library id (its arena source and snapshot in the tooltip);
  - archetype (sorts F, T, D, then by leaf), kind, recipe (the naming-scheme code with its options);
  - regime (the field in the tooltip) and training ply;
  - experiment (a link), line, version (the global league's champions and holdout versions marked), seed;
  - end: plateau, cap, manual-stop, killed or unknown (the end cycle, floor, cap, window and last champion in the
    tooltip); cycles: the end cycle;
  - lineage: its warm start, versions shared with other runs, frozen references;
  - ladder: the latest ladder run's Elo of this version at its training ply; muted with "@v" when the ladder rates
    another version of the line (every ladder entry of the line in the tooltip).
- **Regime**:
  - self-play: a `.sp` line, or a self-play training ply;
  - homogeneous class: trained at one ply in a field of one archetype (`field_class` pure), no random players;
  - diverse class: the same in a field of several archetypes (`field_class` mixed);
  - other, named: observer (a `.obs` line), global league (`global`), old system (plies 1-2 mixed, or random
    players in the field).

  Warm-started lines keep their class; the Start filter (from scratch, warm-started) and the Lineage column tell
  them.
- **Training ply**: index.csv `train_ply`: plies 1 to 4, "plies 1-2 (old system)", or a self-play line's depth.
- **Filters**: Set (fully plateaued, holdouts, other), Type (F, T, D), Leaf (L, N, P), Archetype, Regime, Start,
  Training ply, End. Colour-by starts on Type.
- **A stale index**: a note lists the experiments that ended since the index was built and aren't in it, those it
  lists with end unknown although they have ended, and running ones it doesn't have yet. `python3
  leagues/library/build_index.py` brings it up to date; the page never rebuilds it.

### Play `/play/`

A game against a library model at a chosen ply, added 2026-11-12. The user: "I'd like another option in the menu
that leads to a chess board where I can play against a game against a chosen model in the library at a chosen
ply." It is the one page that does more than read: it runs an engine and saves the games. Its back end is
`leagues/play.py`; its browser files are in `leagues/play-static/`.

- **Opponent**: the Models page's rows (index.csv plus holdout versions).
  - Show: plateaued and holdouts (the default) or all models. Type (F, T, D), Kind, Trained at (ply 1 to 4, other)
    and Find (text) narrow the list.
  - Sort: ladder Elo (the latest ladder run at the training ply, the default), type, training ply or experiment.
  - The chosen row's run, training regime, end and ladder Elo show below the list.
- **Settings**:
  - Ply 1 to 8 (5-8 since 11-12 ~18:12, the user: "select a ply above what we've trained on"; "That's fine it
    olny has to make 1 move every once in a while"): any model at any ply. Timed: plies 5-6 under 0.5 s a move,
    7 up to ~4 s, 8 up to ~20 s (a grow6 DAG or an early position). It starts at the model's training ply when that is 1 to 4. Otherwise it starts at 2 for the old
    system's plies 1-2, at a self-play line's depth, else at 1.
  - You play White, Black or Random.
  - It plays its best move (the log-odds argmax; ties are broken by the game's seed) or as in the arenas (log-odds
    softmax at T 0.02, seeded).
- **The board**: chessground (lichess.org's board, 9.2.1, GPL-3.0) and chess.js (1.4.0, BSD-2-Clause), vendored
  in `play-static/` (README.md there: sources, licences, sha256). Nothing loads from the web.
  - Move by dragging, by clicking the two squares, or by typing SAN (Nf3) or UCI (g1f3).
  - A promotion asks for the piece; Cancel or Escape takes the move back.
  - Buttons: New game, Take back (to before your last move; counted), Flip board, Resign.
- **Rules**: chess.js decides legality and the end of the game. Checkmate, stalemate, insufficient material,
  threefold repetition and the fifty-move rule all end it at once, as in the engine's own games; there is no
  length limit and no clock. The engine's legal moves and game ends were checked against chess.js's in 300
  random games, 93,163 positions, with castling, en passant and every promotion. They agree in all of them.
- **What it shows**:
  - the move list, each model move with the engine's time;
  - the model's calibration (the experiment's own parameters, from the same 600 own games);
  - its White/draw/Black probabilities before and after its move;
  - its search's value of its move and its four best moves.
- **How the model plays**: as an arena player (ARENA.md), `NAME PLY cal:<own games>:<kind>:<model>` with
  `--use-probs`.
  - The model file is the library snapshot's, else the experiment's.
  - Its own calibration games come from the arenas' cache (`arenas/.owncal-cache/`). When the cache doesn't have
    them, they are made once, as `arena.py` makes them, into `play-games/owncal/` (seconds, nice 19, 1 thread).
  - The engine is `models/from-scratch/bin/chessai-play1 play-serve`: hybrid19's tournament code path plus a
    request loop. It starts on the first request at nice 19 with 1 thread, answers one request at a time, keeps up
    to 4 models loaded, and stays up (idle) until the dashboard stops.
  - Its choice was checked against hybrid19 tournaments at plies 1 to 4, in every position
    (`models/from-scratch/play1/summary.txt`).
- **Saved games**: each finished game (mate, draw or resignation) is saved as
  `play-games/<date>-<time>-<id>v<version>-p<ply>-<colour>.pgn`. A game left by New game is not saved.
  - The PGN has the seven standard tags plus Termination, PlyCount, LibraryId, Experiment, Line, Version, Model,
    Archetype, Kind, TrainPly, Ply, Policy, Seed, Takebacks, Engine, ModelFile, ModelMd5, Calibration and Started.
  - The page lists the saved games, served at `/play/games/<file>`.
- **Requests**: POST `/play/api/prepare`, `move`, `eval` and `save`, JSON, each for a row's key (`<id>@<version>`).
  `python3 leagues/play.py resolve|move|legal` makes the same checks from the command line.

### Earlier runs

The Experiments page's Earlier runs section (Home's until 2026-11-12) starts with the global league's row (since
2026-11-12, when the global ladder took the Global slot); its page is still `/global/`. The champion run and the
first run are only linked; this server never loads their dashboard:

- the champion run: `http://127.0.0.1:8765/`;
- the first run: `http://127.0.0.1:8765/first/`.

The section says when nothing listens on that port (read from `/proc/net/tcp`; it never connects).

## What it reads

The files of `FORMATS.md`:

- `index.json`;
- per league: `config.json`, `state.json`, `lines.csv`, `cycles.csv`, `totals.csv`, `rounds.csv`, `events.csv`,
  `history.csv`, `anchors.csv`, `copied.csv`, `observers.csv` (experiments with observers), `batch/batch.json`,
  `cycle-NNN.md`, `driver.log` and `STOP`;
- `/proc/<pid>` for the drivers and the arenas' processes;
- `repeat.log` when it exists (linked from Reports and log as `repeat-log`);
- the arenas (`ARENA.md`): every directory under `arenas/` whose `config.json` has `"kind": "arena"`, and in each
  `config.json`, `state.json`, `calibration.csv`, `report.csv`, `report.md`, `arena.log`, `FAILED` and, in `tour/`,
  `ratings.csv`, `head_to_head.csv` and `games.csv`; also `arenas/pairs/summary.csv`.

For the arenas it imports `arena.py` (`read_ratings`, `score_interval`, `elo_of`) and `arenas/pairs/pairs.py`
(`contrast_ses`, `PAIRS`), without writing bytecode, so its numbers are theirs.

The Play page imports `play.py` (which imports `arena.py` and `leagues.py`, also without bytecode). It reads the
library as the Models page does, the latest ladder run, `play-static/` and the saved games' tags. It writes
`play-games/` (the games and `owncal/`), nothing else.

Reading is cached:

- Each file is re-read only when its mtime, size or inode changes.
- A CSV's trailing partial line is ignored, since the file may be caught mid-append.
- A file that fails to parse keeps its last good reading. The page names it.
- An arena's pair contrasts and head-to-head table are kept until its `tour/` files or `config.json` change.

In the leagues it does not read `tour/`, `runs/`, `owncal/`, `pool/` or the batch's games, except that the nodes
page hashes a line's model file in `runs/` (and the treeview.py it starts reads the line's models and games).

## Format assumptions

These are things `FORMATS.md` leaves open, or that the dashboard takes as given:

- **Standings** come from `history.csv`, so they are always the last completed cycle's. While a cycle runs, they
  are not updated round by round: `tour/ratings.csv` is not read.
- **Line definitions**:
  - `config.json`'s `lines` give the order and the argument lists;
  - `lines.csv` gives the latest status and names. When its `train_args`/`selfplay_args` cells are the only
    source, they are read as a JSON list or as shell words;
  - `state.json`'s `lines` fill in `crowned` and `patience`.
- **Names and labels**: model ids and line names come from `config.json` and `lines.csv`; labels from their
  `label` (else `index.json`'s `labels`). A line shows as its model's label plus the line's option suffix. Version
  names come from the CSVs; only a line with no `version_name` yet gets one made from `state.json`
  (`E.<S|L|G>.<major>.<v>` in experiments, `G.<major>.<v>` in the global league).
- **Overrides**: `config.json`'s `train_overrides` and `selfplay_overrides` (lists of [flag, value]) give the
  sweep factors, one per flag, named without dashes; a line without the flag shows "recipe's". `true` shows as
  "on", `null` as "off".
- **Prices**: a league is priced when its `totals.csv` or `rounds.csv` have `price_s`. A self-play-only
  experiment is one with `selfplay_budget_s` in its config; its lines' target is that fixed budget.
- **Factors**, the classification the champion run's dashboard uses:
  - a line's model comes from its `kind` taken as `--model`, plus `train_args`. `bdt` with `--dag-leaves` and
    `bdd` are DAGs (D);
  - its labels come from `--label-depth`/`--label-result`;
  - a self-play line's depth and move selection come from `selfplay_args`;
  - where the flags don't settle it, the archetype's name is used; failing that, "unclassified".
- **Status** comes from `state.json` (`index.json` when `state.json` has none; the driver writes both, `state.json`
  more often). `running` or `waiting` with no live pid shows as `stopped`. A league on disk that `index.json`
  doesn't list gets its status from its `state.json`, or from whether its pid is alive.
- **Rounds played** in the running cycle: the length of `state.json`'s `current.rounds`, else the rows of
  `rounds.csv` for that cycle.
- **CPU**: totals count the games' own CPU (`tournament_cpu_s`), the rating fit and tables
  (`tournament_proc_cpu_s` minus `tournament_cpu_s`, when the column is there), training, self-play and
  calibration, plus the running cycle's rounds from `rounds.csv`.
- **The phase strip** follows the driver's phase lists (`PHASES_GLOBAL`, `PHASES_EXPERIMENT` in `leagues.py`), with
  `waiting` shown inside copy. "An experiment's last cycle" is a cycle number at or above `config.json`'s `cycles`.
- **Anchors**: intervals are `step` wide from `anchors.lowest` (100 if missing) up, with the margin and
  `out_cycles` from `config.json`'s `anchors`. `available` counts as true for 1/true/True.
- **The batch's "used by"** matches experiments whose `global_source` is this global league's directory, comparing
  their `used_global_rounds` pairs `[global cycle, t]` with `batch.json`'s cycle and each round's `t`.
- **Time left and projected finish**: the remaining cycles times the median wall time of the last five cycles.
- **Events**' values: z for new-champion; the interval's lower bound for anchor-locked (shown as the interval).
  Other values are shown as written.
- **Arenas**:
  - The search goes down at most five levels and stops at an arena; it skips `specs/`, `models/`, `owncal/`,
    `tour/` and `__pycache__/`.
  - Status is `state.json`'s. `running` or `calibration` needs a live process whose working directory or arguments
    are inside the arena's directory (or `arena.py` with its name), or a `state.json` written in the last 120 s;
    otherwise it shows as `stopped`, the reason in the tooltip.
  - Players are each entry at each of its plies (`<name>-<ply>`) plus the references; games are round-robins ×
    n (n − 1).
  - Pairs: pairs.py names a pair arena `pair-<original>` (in `arenas/pairs/<original>/`); a replay of it at ply 1
    is the arena named `ply1-<original>`. Entries are `<tag>.<line>@<v>`, tagged `orig` or `new`, and the two
    champions of a line are matched by the line. An arena outside that naming shows its own plies' contrasts.

## Development

It was developed against a synthetic root made from FORMATS.md, then against the engine's smoke root. Each page was
rendered with `--once` and fetched with curl on port 8766. A headless Chromium run (a temporary profile, a
same-origin proxy) drove the filters, highlighting, sorting, chart toggles and the 30 s refresh.

The Play page was checked the same way: `--once /play/`, curl on a test port, then headless Chromium
(`models/from-scratch/play1/browser-test/ui-test.cjs`). That run moves by drag, click and typing, takes back,
flips, resigns, plays Black through to mate, and promotes by drag (Cancel, Escape, then a knight). It also checks
the saved PGNs.
